package Vixen.Shaders.Pipeline

import Vixen.Shaders.Core
import Vixen.Shaders.Geometry

// Phase 6 of `docs/plan/22-virtualized-geometry.md`: the software raster, and the merge that lands it.
//
// **What this is for, stated as the number it is.** A hardware rasterizer shades in quads, so a
// triangle covering less than one wastes roughly four fifths of the fragments it launches — and a
// virtualized frame's cut is *chosen* to put a cluster's error under a pixel, which is the regime where
// its triangles are sub-pixel by construction. A scanline raster in compute pays per covered pixel
// instead. That is the whole of the trade, and it is a trade rather than a win: at anything above a few
// pixels a triangle the fixed-function path is far faster, which is why the routing threshold is a
// number a host measures and not a constant this file decides.
//
// **Optional hardware, and the gate is real.** Resolving which surface is nearest without a depth
// attachment means one `atomicMax` on a word packing depth above identity, and a 64-bit atomic is
// optional on Vulkan, SM6.6 on D3D12 and absent from WebGPU — so this shader reports `Int64` and
// `Int64Atomics` and `GraphicsDeviceFeatures.HasInt64Atomics` decides whether it is ever created. A
// device without it routes every cluster to `ClusterRaster.rvn`, which needs none of this and is the
// portable baseline for exactly that reason.
//
// **Two dispatches, one entry point, one permutation** — `Culling.rvn`'s arrangement and Raven's rule
// rather than a preference: a shader has one compute stage, so the raster and the merge branch on
// `Merge` and the fold removes the other from each variant. They also want different bindings, and the
// set is the union of both; a host binds all of it either way, which is what `Culling.rvn` already does
// for the object cull and the traversal.
//
// **The merge is what makes the two rasters one picture.** The software pass cannot write the hardware
// depth buffer, so it resolves among *itself* in a packed buffer and the merge then asks, per pixel,
// whether what it found is nearer than what the hardware draw left behind. Both orderings therefore
// come out of a real depth comparison rather than out of which pass ran last. And the merge clears the
// packed buffer as it reads it, which is the frame's whole cost of clearing it: a screen of
// sixty-four-bit words is sixteen megabytes at 1080p, and copying that in every frame to avoid reading
// last frame's answer would cost more than the raster it serves.

/// The packing, the quantization and the bounds the raster, the merge and the host agree on.
struct Software {
    /// What a depth is multiplied by to become the top half of the packed word.
    ///
    /// `2^32 − 256`, which is the largest multiple of 256 below `2^32` and therefore **exactly
    /// representable in a `float`** — the obvious `4294967295` is not, rounds up to `2^32`, and a
    /// conversion of that to a `uint` is undefined in both targets. The eight bits given up are far
    /// below what a `float` depth carries anyway: twenty-four bits of mantissa is the real limit, and
    /// this keeps all of them.
    const val DepthScale = 4294967040f

    /// How far a triangle may reach across the screen before the raster stops following it.
    ///
    /// A loop in a shader has a bound, and this is that bound rather than a policy: the loops break as
    /// soon as the triangle's own box is done, so a sub-pixel triangle costs one iteration and this
    /// number costs nothing. It is larger than any screen dimension the engine targets, so the clamp
    /// is unreachable in a frame and reachable only in a fixture that routes an entire wall to a path
    /// meant for specks.
    const val MaxExtent = 4096

    /// How many rounds of sixty-four lanes cover one cluster's triangles.
    ///
    /// Two, because a cluster holds at most a hundred and twenty-eight of them — the same bound
    /// `Raster.TriangleBits` exists for, and the same one `MeshletBuildSettings.MaxTriangles` produces.
    /// The loop breaks at the cluster's own count, so this is the ceiling and never the cost.
    const val TriangleRounds = 2

    /// One pixel's depth and identity as the word an `atomicMax` resolves.
    ///
    /// **Depth above identity, and unsigned.** Reverse-Z makes the nearer surface the larger number, so
    /// a single `max` picks the winner and the identity rides along as a tie-break nobody looks at. The
    /// top bit of a depth key is data, so a *signed* max would resolve the far half of the range
    /// backwards — right for every scene that never gets there.
    static func Key(depth: float, identity: uint): uint64 {
        return (uint64(uint(saturate(depth) * DepthScale)) << 32) | uint64(identity)
    }

    /// What a pixel nothing covered holds — which is also what the merge writes back.
    static func Empty(): uint64 => uint64(0)

    /// The depth a key carries, back in the range the hardware depth buffer holds.
    static func Depth(key: uint64): float => float(uint(key >> 32)) / DepthScale

    /// The identity it carries.
    static func Identity(key: uint64): uint => uint(key)

    /// One edge function of a triangle, evaluated at a point.
    ///
    /// Twice the signed area of the triangle `(a, b, p)`, positive when `p` is to the left of `a → b`.
    /// The three of them at a pixel centre are its unnormalised barycentrics, which is why the depth
    /// interpolation below is these divided by their sum and nothing else.
    static func Edge(a: float2, b: float2, p: float2): float {
        return (b.x - a.x) * (p.y - a.y) - (b.y - a.y) * (p.x - a.x)
    }

    /// Whether an edge's zero line belongs to this triangle — the top-left rule.
    ///
    /// **Two triangles sharing an edge must cover the pixels on it exactly once**, or a seam is drawn
    /// twice and blends, or drawn not at all and shows the background. The rule is the one every
    /// hardware rasterizer implements: for a counter-clockwise triangle, an edge going strictly left
    /// (a *top* edge) or downward (a *left* edge) owns the pixels exactly on it, and the other two do
    /// not. Only reached when the edge function is exactly zero, so it costs nothing anywhere else.
    static func TopLeft(a: float2, b: float2): bool {
        val dx = b.x - a.x
        val dy = b.y - a.y

        return dy < 0f || (dy == 0f && dx < 0f)
    }
}

shader ClusterSoftwareRaster {
    /// Whether this variant merges the packed buffer into the visibility buffer rather than filling it.
    ///
    /// **A permutation and not a second entry point**, because Raven refuses a second compute stage in
    /// one shader — see `Culling.rvn`, which found the same constraint agreeing with the same intent.
    /// The fold is what keeps the raster free of the merge's full-screen indexing and the merge free of
    /// the raster's page decode.
    [Permutation] val Merge: bool = false

    /// The clusters the traversal accepted, headed by its counters — see `Cull.VisibleBase`.
    ///
    /// This raster reads the *suffix*: word one says how many entries there are, and they descend from
    /// the end of the buffer. The prefix is the hardware raster's, and neither pass has to know that
    /// the other exists.
    var visible: Buffer<uint>

    /// One record per instance, as `Culling.rvn` writes it.
    var instances: Buffer<CullInstance>

    /// Where each cluster's bytes are, indexed as `clusterRecords` is.
    var geometry: Buffer<RasterCluster>

    /// One record per registered mesh.
    var meshes: Buffer<RasterMesh>

    /// Where each page sits in the pool, or `Cull.PageAbsent`. The same table the traversal read.
    var residency: Buffer<uint>

    /// The page pool, as words.
    var pages: Buffer<uint>

    /// Every skinned instance's matrices this frame, back to back.
    var bones: Buffer<BoneMatrix>

    /// Every morphed mesh's shapes, re-indexed by vertex, and the cluster-vertex table that reaches them.
    ///
    /// Three tables in one buffer, because they are read one after another by the same invocation and a
    /// binding costs more than an offset. The first `clusterCount` words are indexed by *global* cluster
    /// — `instance.firstCluster + clusterIndex`, the same index `geometry` takes — and hold where that
    /// cluster's run of source-vertex indices starts. After them come the runs themselves, then per
    /// mesh a prefix over its vertices and its entries, at `mesh.morphRunBase` and `mesh.morphEntryBase`.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **A page vertex does not know which mesh vertex it is**, which is the whole reason this table
    /// exists. A cluster's vertex list is the DAG's — `MeshletMesh.Vertices` — and the page carries only
    /// the position and attributes it copied out. A vertex on a locked boundary appears in a cluster on
    /// each side and at several levels, and every copy of it must pick up the same delta or the cut
    /// cracks; the source index is what makes them the same vertex again.
    var morphs: Buffer<uint>

    /// Every morphed instance's per-shape weights this frame, back to back.
    ///
    /// One buffer for the scene with a per-instance base, exactly as `bones` is, and for the same reason
    /// — there is no per-object command to push a per-object index through.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Pre-multiplied by the shape's quantisation step on the host**: `x` is `weight × positionStep`
    /// and `y` is `weight × normalStep`. Uploading the weight and the step separately would be a second
    /// multiplication for the two processors to round differently, and `MorphKernel.Step` already makes
    /// the argument that one division on the host is one float both then agree about.
    var morphWeights: Buffer<float2>

    /// One packed depth-and-identity word per pixel, row-major.
    ///
    /// A buffer and not an image, and that is what keeps the capability gate to one bit: 64-bit *image*
    /// atomics are a further extension that far less hardware offers, and nothing here wants a texture's
    /// filtering or its layout.
    var depths: RWBuffer<uint64>

    /// The depth the hardware raster left behind, which the merge tests against. `Merge` only.
    var hardwareDepth: Texture2D

    /// The visibility buffer, which the merge writes where the software surface is nearer. `Merge` only.
    ///
    /// A storage image rather than an attachment, because a compute pass has no attachments. The texel
    /// format is part of the declaration in both targets and has to be the one
    /// `GpuClusterRaster.Format` is.
    ///
    /// Four components because a storage image is read and written four at a time whatever its format
    /// says — `r32ui` discards the other three, which is the same shape `VisibilityResolve`'s target has
    /// with the components it does use.
    [Format("r32ui")] var identities: RWTexture2D<uint4>

    /// How many bytes one page occupies in the pool.
    var pageSize: uint

    /// Where this frame's instance records start, in records.
    var instanceBase: uint

    /// Where this frame's palettes start, in matrices, and its slot table, in entries.
    var boneBase: uint

    var residencyBase: uint

    /// Where this frame's blend-shape weights start in `morphWeights`, in entries. `boneBase`'s twin.
    var morphWeightBase: uint

    /// How many pixels the screen is, which is what turns a clip position into a coverage test.
    var screen: int2

    /// The camera. The same matrix `ClusterRaster` is handed, because it draws the same clusters.
    var viewProjection: mat4

    /// One workgroup per software-routed cluster, or one invocation per pixel of the merge.
    ///
    /// The raster's dispatch is indirect — its `x` extent is the traversal's own count word, so the host
    /// never learns how many clusters went this way — and the merge's is the screen, which the host does
    /// know. Sixty-four lanes either way: a cluster's triangles are dealt out to them a stride at a
    /// time, and a merge lane owns one pixel.
    [ComputeShader(64)]
    func Main(
        [Semantic("SV_DispatchThreadID")] id: uint3,
        [Semantic("SV_GroupID")] group: uint3,
        [Semantic("SV_GroupIndex")] lane: uint
    ) {
        if (Merge) {
            Land(int(id.x))
            return
        }

        Rasterize(group.x, lane)
    }

    /// Rasterizes one cluster's triangles into the packed buffer.
    ///
    /// **The lanes share a cluster and not a triangle.** A sub-pixel triangle covers one pixel, so
    /// splitting one across sixty-four lanes would leave sixty-three idle; giving each lane its own
    /// triangle keeps every one of them working and costs nothing in cooperation, because the only
    /// thing two triangles ever have to agree about is the atomic that resolves them.
    func Rasterize(index: uint, lane: uint) {
        if (index >= visible[Cull.VisibleSoftware]) {
            return
        }

        // The suffix, descending from the end. The slot a pixel names is the entry minus the header,
        // which is what makes a software-drawn pixel resolvable by the same reader as a hardware one.
        val entry = visible.Length - 1 - int(index)

        if (entry < Cull.VisibleBase) {
            return
        }

        val packed = visible[entry]
        val slot = uint(entry - Cull.VisibleBase)

        val instance = instances[int(instanceBase) + int(Cull.VisibleInstance(packed))]
        val clusterIndex = Cull.VisibleCluster(packed)
        val record = geometry[int(instance.firstCluster + clusterIndex)]
        val mesh = meshes[int(instance.mesh)]
        val page = residency[int(residencyBase + record.page)]

        // A page that went away between the traversal and this dispatch. It should not happen — a page
        // is evicted only after the frame that drew from it was submitted — and it is checked for the
        // reason `ClusterRaster` checks it: the alternative is drawing another mesh's bytes.
        if (page == Cull.PageAbsent) {
            return
        }

        val start = page * pageSize

        for (i in 0 .. Software.TriangleRounds - 1) {
            val triangle = uint(i) * 64u + lane

            if (triangle >= record.triangleCount) {
                break
            }

            Triangle(slot, triangle, start, record, mesh, instance, clusterIndex)
        }
    }

    /// One triangle, from three page vertices to however many pixels it covers.
    func Triangle(
        slot: uint,
        triangle: uint,
        start: uint,
        record: RasterCluster,
        mesh: RasterMesh,
        instance: CullInstance,
        clusterIndex: uint
    ) {
        val at = start + record.triangleOffset + triangle * 3u

        val c0 = viewProjection * float4(Position(start, record, mesh, instance, clusterIndex, Byte(at)), 1f)
        val c1 = viewProjection * float4(Position(start, record, mesh, instance, clusterIndex, Byte(at + 1u)), 1f)
        val c2 = viewProjection * float4(Position(start, record, mesh, instance, clusterIndex, Byte(at + 2u)), 1f)

        // Nothing here clips, and nothing here has to: the traversal only routes a cluster this way when
        // its whole bound is in front of the near plane — see `Culling.Software`. This is the assertion
        // of that rather than a case, because a corner behind the eye projects to a position that is not
        // wrong so much as meaningless.
        if (c0.w <= Const.Epsilon || c1.w <= Const.Epsilon || c2.w <= Const.Epsilon) {
            return
        }

        val n0 = c0.xyz / c0.w
        val n1 = c1.xyz / c1.w
        val n2 = c2.xyz / c2.w

        val size = float2(float(screen.x), float(screen.y))
        var p0 = Transform.NdcToUv(n0) * size
        var p1 = Transform.NdcToUv(n1) * size
        var p2 = Transform.NdcToUv(n2) * size
        var z0 = n0.z
        var z1 = n1.z
        var z2 = n2.z

        var area = Software.Edge(p0, p1, p2)

        // A degenerate triangle covers nothing, and the surplus corners of a short cluster are exactly
        // that — `ClusterRaster` gets the same case for free, as a triangle the hardware discards.
        if (area == 0f) {
            return
        }

        // **Wound to face the same way, not culled.** `ClusterRaster`'s pipeline is two-sided and the
        // normal cone has already rejected the clusters that face away as a whole, so a back-facing
        // triangle inside a surviving cluster is drawn on both paths or on neither. Flipping it here is
        // what lets one top-left rule serve both windings.
        if (area < 0f) {
            val swap = p1
            p1 = p2
            p2 = swap

            val swapZ = z1
            z1 = z2
            z2 = swapZ

            area = -area
        }

        val identity = Raster.Pack(slot, triangle)

        // The box, in pixels, snapped outward and clamped to the screen. A pixel is tested at its
        // centre, which is where `VisibilityResolve` reconstructs barycentrics from — the two have to be
        // the same point or the attributes belong to a neighbouring pixel.
        val x0 = max(int(floor(min(min(p0.x, p1.x), p2.x))), 0)
        val y0 = max(int(floor(min(min(p0.y, p1.y), p2.y))), 0)
        val x1 = min(int(ceil(max(max(p0.x, p1.x), p2.x))), screen.x - 1)
        val y1 = min(int(ceil(max(max(p0.y, p1.y), p2.y))), screen.y - 1)

        for (row in 0 .. Software.MaxExtent - 1) {
            val y = y0 + row

            if (y > y1) {
                break
            }

            for (column in 0 .. Software.MaxExtent - 1) {
                val x = x0 + column

                if (x > x1) {
                    break
                }

                Cover(float2(float(x) + 0.5f, float(y) + 0.5f), p0, p1, p2, z0, z1, z2, area, x, y, identity)
            }
        }
    }

    /// Tests one pixel centre against one triangle and, if it is inside, resolves it against whatever
    /// else has been there.
    func Cover(
        point: float2,
        p0: float2,
        p1: float2,
        p2: float2,
        z0: float,
        z1: float,
        z2: float,
        area: float,
        x: int,
        y: int,
        identity: uint
    ) {
        val e0 = Software.Edge(p1, p2, point)
        val e1 = Software.Edge(p2, p0, point)
        val e2 = Software.Edge(p0, p1, point)

        if (!Inside(e0, p1, p2) || !Inside(e1, p2, p0) || !Inside(e2, p0, p1)) {
            return
        }

        // `z/w` is affine in screen space, which is the one attribute a rasterizer may interpolate
        // without the perspective divide the resolve does for everything else. The weights are the edge
        // functions over their sum, which is the area.
        val depth = (e0 * z0 + e1 * z1 + e2 * z2) / area

        // One atomic, and the whole reason phase 6 needed a 64-bit one. With thirty-two bits the word
        // holds a depth or an identity, and the alternative is two passes over the same triangles: a
        // depth pass by `atomicMin` and an identity pass testing equality against it.
        val previous = atomicMax(depths[y * screen.x + x], Software.Key(depth, identity))
    }

    /// Whether a pixel is on the inner side of one edge, with the ties decided by the top-left rule.
    func Inside(edge: float, a: float2, b: float2): bool {
        if (edge > 0f) {
            return true
        }

        if (edge < 0f) {
            return false
        }

        return Software.TopLeft(a, b)
    }

    /// Merges one pixel of the packed buffer into the visibility buffer, and clears it.
    ///
    /// **The comparison is against the hardware depth and not against a flag.** A frame where some
    /// clusters went each way has to resolve between the two, and the only thing that can decide it is
    /// the depth both of them computed — so the hardware pass writes its depth as it always did and this
    /// asks whether the software surface is in front of it. Equal loses, which makes the tie
    /// deterministic and gives the fixed-function depth the last word on the pixels it drew.
    ///
    /// **And it clears as it reads.** The next frame's raster wants an empty buffer, and this pass
    /// visits every pixel of it anyway.
    func Land(index: int) {
        if (index >= screen.x * screen.y) {
            return
        }

        val key = depths[index]
        depths[index] = Software.Empty()

        if (key == Software.Empty()) {
            return
        }

        val pixel = int2(index % screen.x, index / screen.x)

        if (Software.Depth(key) <= hardwareDepth.Load(int3(pixel.x, pixel.y, 0)).x) {
            return
        }

        val identity = Software.Identity(key)

        identities.Store(pixel, uint4(identity, 0u, 0u, 0u))
    }

    /// One page vertex's world position — `ClusterRaster`'s vertex stage, with the attributes it does
    /// not need left out.
    ///
    /// **The transform has to agree with the raster's and the resolve's bit for bit.** All three place
    /// the same vertex, and a disagreement between any two of them lands a surface in one place and its
    /// material in another. The integer grid addition is what makes a locked cluster boundary decode
    /// identically from both sides of it, and it is an addition here for the same reason it is one
    /// there.
    func Position(
        start: uint,
        record: RasterCluster,
        mesh: RasterMesh,
        instance: CullInstance,
        clusterIndex: uint,
        local: uint
    ): float3 {
        val at = start + record.vertexOffset + local * record.vertexStride

        val x = record.origin.x + int(Short(at))
        val y = record.origin.y + int(Short(at + 2u))
        val z = record.origin.z + int(Short(at + 4u))

        var position = mesh.quantizationOrigin + float3(float(x), float(y), float(z)) * mesh.quantizationStep

        // ⚠ Before the skin, which is the order the classic path has by construction — a delta is
        // authored in the mesh's own space, so a skinned rest vertex with a delta added lands a jaw's
        // displacement in the head's bind pose rather than in its pose.
        if (mesh.morphRunBase != RasterMesh.NoMorphs && instance.firstWeight != Cull.NoWeights) {
            position = position + Morphed(instance, mesh, clusterIndex, local).position
        }

        if (mesh.influenceOffset != RasterMesh.NoInfluences && instance.firstBone != Cull.NoBones) {
            position = Math.TransformPoint(Skin(at + mesh.influenceOffset, instance), position)
        }

        return instance.position + position * instance.scale
    }

    /// What one page vertex's blend shapes moved it by, gathered.
    ///
    /// **`MorphIndex.Apply` transliterated**, and held to it the way `Skin` is held to `Skinning`: the
    /// host writes the arithmetic once and `MorphIndexTests` holds *that* to `MorphKernel`, which the
    /// device's own scatter kernel is already held to. Three parties, one definition.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **A vertex no shape moves costs two reads and returns zero**, which is most of a face and all
    /// of a body. That is what the prefix table buys and why the entries are indexed by vertex rather
    /// than searched per shape.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **The normal is not renormalised here or anywhere after.** A shape may cancel one exactly and
    /// there is no direction left to recover; `VisibilityResolve` `SafeNormalize`s at 1e-4, which
    /// answers zero rather than a NaN. `MorphKernel` makes the argument at length.
    func Morphed(instance: CullInstance, mesh: RasterMesh, clusterIndex: uint, local: uint): MorphDelta {
        // ⚠ Accumulated into locals and assigned to the record once, rather than read-modify-written
        // through the record's own fields. `delta.position = delta.position + …` inside the loop
        // compiled, drew, and kept the value it was initialised with — every table correct, every
        // record correct, the loop body demonstrably running, and a plane at rest. A struct member is
        // not a place to accumulate in.
        var position = float3(0f, 0f, 0f)
        var normal = float3(0f, 0f, 0f)

        // The cluster's own run of source indices, then this corner's entry in it. Two indirections,
        // because a page vertex carries no identity of its own — see `morphs`.
        val run = morphs[int(mesh.morphClusterBase + clusterIndex)]
        val source = morphs[int(run + local)]

        val start = morphs[int(mesh.morphRunBase + source)]
        val last = morphs[int(mesh.morphRunBase + source + 1u)]

        // ⚠ A counted loop with a break rather than a `while`, and the bound is not a guess.
        // `MorphIndex.Build` refuses a mesh whose vertex is moved by more than `Morph.MaxShapes`
        // shapes, naming the vertex — so this bound is unreachable rather than a truncation nobody
        // would see. `ClusterCulling`'s light loop is the same shape for the same reason.
        for (i in 0 .. Morph.MaxShapes - 1) {
            val entry = start + uint(i)

            if (entry >= last) {
                break
            }

            val at = mesh.morphEntryBase + entry * Morph.EntryWords
            val shape = morphs[int(at)]
            val weight = morphWeights[int(morphWeightBase + instance.firstWeight + shape)]

            val first = morphs[int(at + 1u)]
            val second = morphs[int(at + 2u)]
            val third = morphs[int(at + 3u)]

            // Six shorts across three words: the position occupies the first and a half, the normal the
            // last and a half. `MorphKernel.Pack` decides that layout and this reads it — a reader that
            // took both triples from the same half would read the position's z as the normal's x.
            position = position + float3(Morph.Low(first), Morph.High(first), Morph.Low(second)) * weight.x
            normal = normal + float3(Morph.High(second), Morph.Low(third), Morph.High(third)) * weight.y
        }

        var delta: MorphDelta
        delta.position = position
        delta.normal = normal

        return delta
    }

    /// The one matrix a vertex's four influences blend to. `ClusterRaster.Skin`, verbatim.
    ///
    /// The third copy of these ten lines, and the duplication is the one `Skinning` already argues for:
    /// indexing a palette is an access chain in the shader that declares the binding and a
    /// sixteen-kilobyte copy anywhere else. `SkinnedClusterTests` compares all three character for
    /// character, because a tolerance there would be a tolerance for one of them fetching bone 1 where
    /// another fetches bone 2.
    func Skin(at: uint, instance: CullInstance): mat4 {
        val indices = Word(at)
        val start = int(boneBase + instance.firstBone)

        var palette: BonePalette
        palette.bone0 = bones[start + int(Influences.Bone(indices, 0))].matrix
        palette.bone1 = bones[start + int(Influences.Bone(indices, 1))].matrix
        palette.bone2 = bones[start + int(Influences.Bone(indices, 2))].matrix
        palette.bone3 = bones[start + int(Influences.Bone(indices, 3))].matrix

        return Skinning.BlendMatrix(palette, Influences.Weights(Word(at + 4u)))
    }

    /// One whole word out of the pool, at a four-byte-aligned byte offset.
    func Word(at: uint): uint => pages[int(at >> 2)]

    /// One byte out of the pool, at a byte offset.
    func Byte(at: uint): uint {
        val word = pages[int(at >> 2)]
        return (word >> int((at & 3u) * 8u)) & 0xFFu
    }

    /// One unsigned short out of the pool, at a byte offset.
    func Short(at: uint): uint {
        val word = pages[int(at >> 2)]
        return (word >> int((at & 2u) * 8u)) & 0xFFFFu
    }
}
